nt for her help before she met with  the Dagda Morgan had already visited Lou  one of the fiercest warriors within the  two @nn and she had chanted a battle  incitement that would bolster him and  rally his men we can see here that  Morgan is clearly in favor of the battle  and has already taken steps to help her  people there are many who saw Morgan as  a goddess of sexual desires but this  story allows us to interpret her in a  very different way if anything it is the  Dagda who is the god of sex as he's  closely associated fertility and it's  very clear that regardless of what the  two did during their meeting the Morgan  had every intention in helping him I  mentioned earlier that some people  believed that the two would come  together once a year and my  interpretation of these events is that  the  who met as they normally would but this  time it was a meeting of War and Morgan  shared her strategic knowledge of the  Dagda so she could help ensure that her  people were victorious in battle the  Morgan's lineage much like a lot of  Irish and Celtic mythology can vary as  there are very few written accounts that  we can go by the medieval Irish book  labore kabbalah ln the book of the takin  of ireland as we now refer to it  mentions that the irish mother goddess  ernest was the mother of morgan as well  as a trio of sister she was associated  with despite their annual meetings there  is no real mention of the Dagda and the  Morgan ever having a child the one piece  of hazy information we have suggest that  they may have had a child by the name of  Adair de Morgan's appearance and  artistic representations remain quite  similar despite how much we actually  know she was often seen as a beautiful  young woman with long flowing dark hair  with her being so heavily associated  with death her clothing was often black  she was known to take the form of a crow  or a raven both of them being carrion  birds and once again tying in to her  association with death it's likely that  the Morgan would have been seen as  strikingly beautiful to the Irish and  Celtic people but she would have also  painted an extremely intimidating figure  as seen the goddess was considered a bad  omen it's fairly difficult to draw too  many conclusions or even to summarize  the Morgan because of the lack of  knowledge and ambiguity that surrounds  her we know that she's an extremely  ancient deity that first appeared in  Irish mythology and then later became an  iconic figure in Celtic mythology she's  often described as quite a vengeful  deity one that would not hesitate to  take the life of someone who has wronged  her we do somewhat see this in the  Ulster cycle when the heroku colon  rejects the Morgans romantic approaches  she swears revenge upon him and  eventually appears to him washing his  armor and delivering him the omen of  death it's likely that the Irish and  Celtic people considered her to be an  extremely intelligent and fierce  individual when I first heard of the  Morgan I instantly thought of the  character from the first Dragon Age game  with the same name the to do somewhat  have similar personalities and her  appearance does resemble what I somewhat  described earlier the character was also  capable of shape-shifting and I'd like  to think that she was influenced by  either Irish or Celtic mythology  there are so many different  interpretations of the Morrigan that I  love to see how you see her do you  consider her a part of a trio of  goddesses similar to how we see a karta  in Greek mythology or do you see her as  a single entity were you familiar with  the tribal supernatural beings that she  belonged to or would you like me to  cover more of the supernatural beings  that appeared in Irish mythology let me  know in the comments below as always  I've been your host mythology and  fiction explained
 

